window.__labLoaded({"id":"6.2","title":"p-values and Significance","goal":"Turn the bottling plant's z statistic into one-sided and two-sided p-values, and see what alpha = 0.05 means as a cut-off.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"From the data to a z statistic","text":"The plant claims bottles average 500 mL (sigma = 12 mL known). We weighed 64 bottles and got a mean of 503.6 mL. The z statistic says how many standard errors the sample mean sits from the claim.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nmu0, sigma, n, xbar = 500, 12, 64, 503.6\n\nse = sigma / np.sqrt(n)\nz = (xbar - mu0) / se\n\nprint(f'standard error = {se:.2f}')\nprint(f'z = {z:.2f}')","out":"standard error = 1.50\nz = 2.40","figs":[]},{"title":"One-sided and two-sided p-values","text":"norm.sf (the 'survival function') gives the area in the right tail beyond z. The two-sided p-value counts both tails, so it is double the one-sided value. A left tail uses norm.cdf.","code":"p_right = stats.norm.sf(z)         # area beyond +z\np_left = stats.norm.cdf(z)         # area below z\np_two = 2 * stats.norm.sf(abs(z))  # both tails\n\nprint(f'right-tailed p = {p_right:.4f}')\nprint(f'left-tailed p  = {p_left:.4f}')\nprint(f'two-tailed p   = {p_two:.4f}')","out":"right-tailed p = 0.0082\nleft-tailed p  = 0.9918\ntwo-tailed p   = 0.0164","figs":[]},{"title":"Alpha as a cut-off (critical values)","text":"Alpha = 0.05 is the surprise threshold. norm.ppf turns it into critical z values that cut off the rejection region. Comparing |z| with the critical value gives the same decision as comparing p with alpha.","code":"alpha = 0.05\ncrit_two = stats.norm.ppf(1 - alpha / 2)   # two-tailed cut-off\ncrit_right = stats.norm.ppf(1 - alpha)     # right-tailed cut-off\n\nprint(f'two-tailed critical z = +/-{crit_two:.3f}')\nprint(f'right-tailed critical z = {crit_right:.3f}')\nprint('p <= alpha?     ', p_two <= alpha)\nprint('|z| > critical?', abs(z) > crit_two)","out":"two-tailed critical z = +/-1.960\nright-tailed critical z = 1.645\np <= alpha?      True\n|z| > critical? True","figs":[]},{"title":"Draw the tail areas","text":"A picture of the p-value: the red areas are the two tails beyond +z and -z. Their combined area is the two-sided p-value, and they are tiny compared with the whole curve.","code":"import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nx = np.linspace(-4, 4, 400)\nplt.figure(figsize=(6, 3.4))\nplt.plot(x, stats.norm.pdf(x), color='black')\nfor side in (x >= abs(z), x <= -abs(z)):\n    plt.fill_between(x[side], stats.norm.pdf(x[side]), color='tab:red', alpha=0.5)\nplt.axvline(crit_two, linestyle='--', color='gray')\nplt.axvline(-crit_two, linestyle='--', color='gray')\nplt.title(f'z = {z:.2f}: two-sided p = {p_two:.4f} (red); dashed = critical values')\nplt.xlabel('z')\nplt.show()","out":"","figs":["6.2-4.png"]}]},"r":{"code":"xbar <- 503.6; mu0 <- 500; sigma <- 12; n <- 64\nz <- (xbar - mu0) / (sigma / sqrt(n))\nz\npnorm(z, lower.tail = FALSE)   # right-tailed p\npnorm(z)                       # left-tailed p\n2 * pnorm(-abs(z))             # two-tailed p\nqnorm(c(0.975, 0.95))          # critical values","expect":"z = 2.4; right-tailed p = 0.0082; left-tailed p = 0.9918; two-tailed p = 0.0164; critical values 1.960 and 1.645","note":"pnorm gives the area to the left, so lower.tail = FALSE gives the right tail. qnorm is the inverse and returns the critical values."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable (use the CDF.NORMAL and IDF.NORMAL functions)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / xbar mu0 sigma n.\nBEGIN DATA\n503.6 500 12 64\nEND DATA.\nCOMPUTE z = (xbar - mu0) / (sigma / SQRT(n)).\nCOMPUTE p_right = 1 - CDF.NORMAL(z, 0, 1).\nCOMPUTE p_left = CDF.NORMAL(z, 0, 1).\nCOMPUTE p_two = 2 * (1 - CDF.NORMAL(ABS(z), 0, 1)).\nCOMPUTE crit_two = IDF.NORMAL(0.975, 0, 1).\nCOMPUTE crit_right = IDF.NORMAL(0.95, 0, 1).\nLIST.","note":"SPSS has no menu for a z-test with known sigma, so the numbers are computed with normal-curve functions. LIST prints z, the p-values and the critical values (shown with SPSS's default 2 decimals; double-click the output to see more)."},"stata":{"code":"display (503.6 - 500) / (12 / sqrt(64))\ndisplay 1 - normal(2.4)         // right-tailed p\ndisplay normal(2.4)             // left-tailed p\ndisplay 2 * (1 - normal(2.4))   // two-tailed p\ndisplay invnormal(0.975)        // two-tailed critical value\ndisplay invnormal(0.95)         // right-tailed critical value","note":"normal() is the area to the left of z and invnormal() is its inverse. The first line computes z = 2.4, which is then typed into the other lines."},"try":["Change xbar to 502 and predict z and the two-sided p-value (the lesson says p is about 0.18), then run it.","Set z = 1.80 by hand (z = 1.8) and compare the right-tailed and two-tailed p-values. Which one passes alpha = 0.05?"]});
